

Version 4.1
===========

As we continue to modify the model and attempt new analyses, the number of
output sheets and columns per sheet continues to increase. It seems unlikely
that we will be able to remove any of the existing columns in the near future.
For this reason, we have been exploring new methods for tracking outcomes that
hopefully satisfy the following criteria:

  - Fast
  - Uncomplicated (i.e. require fewer lines of code than existing method)
  - Easy to consume in scripts and by hand for further analysis
  - Small file size
  - Easy to move
  - Fault-tolerant (e.g. if a cluster node fails during simulation, it should be
evident)

These turned out to be a difficult set of criteria to satisfy. The initial idea
was to use an XLSX library to create an XML based spreadsheet. XLSX uses
compression, so the file size would be smaller, it would create a single file
that could be read on any computer with Excel. It would also allow us to freeze
panes automatically (an action we normally have to do every time we open a
CSV). The problem with this idea is consuming the data in scripts. Any time we
add a new statistic, we have to change our scripts as the columns are shifted.
The sheets are also cumbersome to explore in Excel.

Next, we tried writing a table of events from which all outcomes could be
derived. For example, entity 1 is born at time 512, becomes high risk at time
716, and dies at time 1181. From this we can calculate the number of people
alive at any point in time, the number of high risk alive at each month, etc.
This idea works, but there are too many events to track all of the necessary
statistics for a large population. With 100,000 people over 1200 months, tests
showed that there would be around half a billion events.
A compromise of these ideas is to write summary statistics for each month to a
database. This database could be queried to create summary tables. SQLite
database store data much more compactly than a plain text file (if a certain
statistic was zero for a given month, it doesn’t need to be written, saving
space). This was the method that was chosen for the time being. The database
replaces all results files including the CEPAC log and batch statistics. A
single database is created for each batch (a batch is a set of XMLs in the same
folder). A post processing script produces summaries as needed from this
database using queries. As an example, we can produce the number of HIV+
females for each month starting with month 600 stratified by HVL stratum and
export to CSV.


Version 4.0
===========

Miscellaneous Changes:
----------------------

- Removed possibility to have CSW proportion differ in initial population and
newborn population as these parameters were never different. These have been
unified into one parameter called “chanceBecomeSexWorker” defined for each
entity.

- The number of sequenced CEPAC IN files or ART rollout files is now unlimited
(originally restricted to 5 and 12 respectively).

- Most outcomes stratified on gender are now stratified on entity type so that
MSMs and MSMWs can be differentiated from heterosexual males.

- New columns at the end of Partnership trace summarize how often MSMWs, MSMs,
and women were selected for a partnership. This will be used to ensure that
certain males (of which some can now both select and be selected for
partnerships) are not selected an extreme number of times.


MSM Implementation – Initial Infection Definitions:
---------------------------------------------------
Initial prevalent cases were originally defined by rigid age- and risk-groups.
Now initial prevalent cases have been generalized so they can be as specific or
vague as desired. For each “bucket”, we can define an optional demographic
profile with wildcards, an optional open or closed age range, and an optional
risk-group along with the number of infections in that specified bucket to be
applied randomly among matches. 
A demographic profile looks like 


<sexual-activity-status>:<gender>:<sexual-orientation>:<relationship-status>:<employment>. 

Each of these can be a specific value or a wildcard in the form of an asterisk
(i.e. SA:FEMALE:HETERO:*:*). For each of these “population queries” we specify
a target number of infections to be assigned randomly among all those matching
the specified criteria.
Example - on month 600, randomly infect 2 sexually-active heterosexual non-CSW
high-risk males between the ages of 17 and 19 years disregarding
relationship-status and 3 high-risk people of any kind:

<initialInfections>
  <delay>600</delay>
    <profile bucket="SA:MALE:HETERO:*:NON_CSW" risk="high" age-range="17-19">2
    </profile>
  <profile risk="high">3</profile>
</initialInfections>


MSM Implementation – Sex-based transmission parameters:
-------------------------------------------------------

Transmission coefficients have been moved out of individual entity types and
into separate section. As MSMWs and heterosexual males can both form
partnerships with females with presumably the same transmission coefficients,
it makes sense to separate these so that all entity types can use them based
simply on the gender of the participants (and eventually on MSM positioning,
not yet implemented). This new section is called “transmissionCoefficients” in
the XML.

MSM Implementation – Birth Proportions:
---------------------------------------

Instead of “proportionMale” in population parameters, the makeup of newborn
entities each month is defined as proportion of total newborns by entity type.
For example, a birth rate of 0.1 with a population size of 1000 yields 100 new
entities in that month. If the birth proportions looks like “male=0.3,
msmw=0.1, msm=0.2, female=0.4”, there will be 30 new males, 10 new MSMWs, 20
new MSMs, and 40 new females. This same system is used in defining the initial
state and population sizes of each age bucket. These two changes allow MSMs to
be present in the population. 

Extra care should be taken when the birth rate is low and the population size
is small (i.e. only a few newborns per month). If the birth rate is 0.001 and
population size is 1000, there will be one birth per month, and this birth will
always be male due to rounding and internal event ordering.


MSM Implementation – MSM Partnerships:
--------------------------------------

Added four new partnership types: “SteadyMSM”, “RegularMSM”, “CasualMSM”,
“CSWMSM”. These new partnerships can only be formed between two homosexual
males (MSMs or MSMWs). MSMWs can form any of the now eight possible partnership
types. MSMs can form only these four new partnership types. Heterosexual males
continue to form “Steady”, “Regular”, “Casual”, and “CSW” partnerships with
heterosexual females as before. These existing partnership types now refer
solely to the heterosexual partnership variant.
In the input parameters, MSMWs should have all eight partnership types. MSMs
should have only the four new MSM partnership types. Heterosexual males should
continue to have only the four heterosexual partnership types. An exception
will prevent the model from running if these conditions are not satisfied.


MSM Implementation – MSMs & MSMWs:
----------------------------------

Two new entity types have been added: MSMW (man who has sex with men and women)
and MSM (man who has sex with (only) men). Both entity types are defined as
having their demographic “sexual-orientation” set to “homosexual”. Each of
these entity classes corresponds to an entity type as follows:
“male”=exclusively heterosexual male, “msmw”=MSMW, “msm”=MSM,
“female”=heterosexual female. To reduce confusion between “male” the gender and
“male” the entity type, these entity types will generally be displayed as
“Male:Hetero”, “Male:MSMW”, “Male:MSM”, and “Female” respectively. We may wish
to rename these to reduce the chance of confusion.

For information on how MSMs and MSMWs are actually added to the population, see
section “4.0 – MSM Implementation – Birth Proportions”.

Note for programmers: For the time being, Male (heterosexual), MSM, and MSMW
are each separately derived from Entity. They share the majority of their
functionality, so they should be merged into a sexual-orientation-independent
“Male” which each subtype derives from. An even better solution would be the
component-based system discussed in Fall 2014 wherein Entity is the only entity
type and entities in the model are a combination of behaviors such as:
selects-partners, selected-for-partnerships, can-become-sex-worker, etc. See
component-model.docx for more information.


Version 3.6
===========

New Cost Discounting Method:
----------------------------

Changed the way cost discounting is handled in the model. Previously, all CEPAC
patients were initialized with a discount factor of 1. Each month this factor
would be reduced by a multiplicative factor defined in the CEPAC IN file. This
has the effect of reducing the weight of costs that occur further in the future
(relative to the start of the simulation). This works fine in CEPAC which
ignores simulation time (all entities are born at time 0), but entities born
after the start of the simulation in the CDM were also initialized to a
discount factor of 1. This inflated the weight of their costs and prevented
discounting from working as expected. Now, discount factor is defined as
relative to the start of the model for all entities. This affects costs
calculated in CEPAC (run within CDM) as well.

Technical Changes:
------------------

- Fixed bug that disabled ShiftedOutcomes if month of 1990 was set to
0.

- Fixed bug in interventions targeting behavioural parameters. These
interventions were     
ignoring the male’s risk so males would take low or high risk behaviors
randomly.

- Prevented males from redrawing parameters set during interventions if they
didn’t change the underlying distribution.

- Got rid of git submodules as they were causing updates to go slowly.
Dependencies are now stored in folder in the repository’s root directory called
third-party. This directory is not tracked by version control. Generally it
will contain symbolic links to the libraries needed by the CDM such as boost,
Cepac, etc.

- Add new option for target group eligibility for HIV status: positive. This
will match any person in the population with a non-negative HIV status without
having to match each individual HIV status (e.g. UNOBSERVED_ACUTE).

- A simpler and more correct (i.e. selects uniformly from group members) method
for assigning target group members to partitions was implemented.

- Added parameters to each entity in the simulation so they can track their
individual CEPAC and CDM costs for the month. These are recorded both
discounted and undiscounted. These numbers along with discounted and
undiscounted life months are displayed in the InterventionOutcomes file
aggregated by group partition. Note: due to timing quirks, this number is
delayed by one month.


Version 3.5
===========

New JSON output of transmission data:
-------------------------------------

To help visualize the transmission networks, a new trace file has been added to
the model. This file is in JSON format. It can be in one of two structures
(hierarchical or adjacency list) or disabled. A description of each type is
given below.
Hierarchical: Each person holds an array of each person he or she infects who
in turns holds an array of the same. In this way, each person is nested inside
whoever was the first to be infected.

{
  { “id” : 1, “infected” : [
    {“id” : 2}
  ]}
}

Adjacency list: The transmission graph is given in two parts. The vertices are
an array of people with their demographic characteristics. Next is an array of
objects, edges, representing each transmission where numbers are the respective
indices of the infectors and infectees in the “vertices” array.

{
  “vertices” : [
    { “id” : 1 },
    { “id” : 2 }
  ],
  “edges” : [
    {“infector” : 0, “infectee” : 1}
  ]
}


New Interventions:
------------------

Three new interventions have been added to the model: delaySexualActivity,
overrideCondomUseChance, and partnershipRejectionChance. Additionally, existing
interventions have been modified to be easier and more logical to use. The
previous mapping of template parameters to interventions has been replaced by a
set of interventions identified by category. They are as follows:

| Intervention               | Parameter1        | Parameter2    | Value
+----------------------------+-------------------+---------------+---------------------
| birthRate                  | (none)            |               | >=0
| proportionMale             | (none)            |               | 0-1
| proportionCircumcised      | (none)            |               | 0-1
| proportionHighRisk         | gender            | employment    | 0-1
| rolloutEligibility         | criterion         | parameter     | <depends on parameter>
| circumcise                 | (none)            |               | (none)
| delaySexualActivity        | (none)            |               | 0-inf. (in months)
| chanceBecomeSexWorker      | gender            |               | 0-1
| transmissionCoefficient    | gender            | hvl           | 0-1
| averageYearsYounger        | partnership-type  |               | normal dist.
| partnerAcquistionRate      | partnership-type  | risk          | normal dist.
| coitalEventsPerMonth       | partnership-type  | risk          | poisson dist.
| chanceCondomUse            | partnership-type  | risk          | normal dist.
| partnershipDuration        | partnership-type  | risk          | shifted normal dist.
| overrideChanceCondomUse    | (none)            |               | 0-1 (-1 to disable)
| partnershipRejectionChance | Partnership-type  | risk          | 0-1 (0 to disable)

Some interventions require additional parameters to be applied (columns
Parameter1 and Parameter2), particularly those relating to sexual behavior.
These are categorical parameters and are defined in the proceeding table:

| Parameter        | Possible values (comma separated)
+------------------+------------------------------------------------
| hvl              | -1 (or uninfected), 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 (or 
|                  | primary), 8 (or late-stage)                  
| employment       | csw, non-csw
| gender           | male, female
| criterion        | oi-hist, cd4, cd4-oi-hist, hvl, cd4-hvl
| parameter        | rank, oi#, numOIs, cd4Lwr, cd4Upp, hvlLwr, hvlUpp
| partnership-type | steady, regular, casual, csw
| risk             | high, low



Target Groups:
--------------
The concept of “target groups” has been added to the model. These are similar
to groups in experimental studies. If we assume we are replicating an
observational study, this might also be called a cohort. A group is all people
who meet a set of eligibility criteria. Criteria are similar to those
previously used as targets for targeted interventions. They are as follows:
| Attribute              |  Value                                |
+------------------------+---------------------------------------+
| age                    | a particular age in months or a       |
|                        | range of the form “lower-upper”       |
|                        | including endpoints also in months    |
| gender                 | male, female                          |
| sexual-orientation     | hetero, homo                          |
| sexual-activity-status | active, not-active                    |
| relationship-status    | single, non-single                    |
| employment             | csw, non-csw                          |
| hiv-status             | negative, observed-acute,             |
|                        | observed-chronic, observed-latestage, |
|                        | unobserved-acute, unobserved-chronic, |
|                        | unobserved-latestage                  |
| treatment-status       | treated, untreated                    |
| circumcised            | true, false                           |
| risk-group             | high, low                             |

To allow for more realistic experimental designs, target groups are further
divided into partitions. Every member of the population who meets the group
criteria are randomly assigned one of the partitions. Each partition receives a
defined percentage of the newly joined people.

Every partition can have zero or more interventions applied to it. For now,
interventions are applied immediately when someone joins the partition. Once
someone has joined a partition, they remain in that partition until death even
if they become ineligible later.

A full definition of a group with a control and treatment partition can be
found below. The syntax for interventions is defined in the next section.

<target-groups>
  <target-group id=”group1”>
    <enrollment-time>600</enrollment-time>
    <eligibility>
      <gender>male</gender>
      <circumcised>false</circumcised>
    </eligibility>
    <partitions>
      <partition id=”treatment”>
        <proportion>0.5</proportion>
        <trace>true</trace>
        <interventions>
          </circumcise>
        </interventions>
      </partition>
    <partition id=”treatment”>
      <proportion>0.5</proportion>
      <trace>true</trace>
    </partition>
  </target-group>
</target-groups>



Technical Changes:
------------------
- Added a new component, RunTimePredictor, which dynamically fits a polynomial
curve to the population so far to predict future population size. By comparing
the integral of the population in the past to the integral of the predicted
curve, we can now estimate the time until a run finishes more accurately (e.g.
timestep 600 is often less than 33% completed in terms of time due to
exponential population growth).

- Corrected any warnings detected in GCC and Clang compilers.

- Fixed bug that prevented a random seed from being used (random number
generator would be started in an undefined state as
RandomNumberGenerator::reset was not being called when fixedSeed was set to -1.
This also prevented the true seed from being written to the infections
file.

- Continued improving const-correctness.

- Removed default constructor for Person.

- Allowed generation of infection to be recorded for generations greater than
5.

- Removed unused method, Person::fling.

- Continued converting arrays for sexual behavior to maps in preparation for
new partnership types for MSM model.

- Added template method for DemographicProfile,
DemographicProfile::getDemographicProfileVal(), to reduce the number of
casts.

- Prevent males from being circumcised twice.


Version 3.4
===========

Discovered Bugs:
----------------
RandomNumberGenerator::randInt(int max) and RandomNumberGenerator::randInt(int
min, int max) appear to have a bug that skews results away from endpoints. This
is due to the fact that it uses rounding to force a random float into an
integer. Numbers from min to min+0.5 are rounded down to min. Numbers from
min+0.5 to min+1.5 are rounded to min+1. Numbers from min+1.5 to min+2.5 are
rounded to min. Numbers from max-0.5 to max are rounded up to max. It can
therefore be seen that the endpoints, min and max, are half as likely to be
returned from these functions as every other intermediate number in the range.

This bug appears to affect the model in the following locations (the expected
result follows each):

- BucketAge::drawMember (line 196) - First and last people in the age bucket
are less likely to be picked

- BucketSexualMixing::getRandomPerson (line 313) - First and last people in
sexual mixing bucket are less likely to be picked, could cause boundary
condition leading to choosing slightly younger people.

- Population::GeneratePerson (line 1043) - Less likely to generate person at
each extreme of age bucket ages

- Population::ApplyARTRollout (line 1558) - First and last people that became
eligible for ART are less likely to be picked to start treatment.

- SexualPartnership::SexualPartnership (line 38) - Partnerships with duration
greater than or equal to 1 will be less likely to be 1 month or max duration.

The bug will remain for now to ensure reproducibility. It should be addressed
in future versions.

Another bug was discovered in the reading of XML files. It appears that regular
acts, a parameter varied in calibration has been being truncated (rounded down)
to an integer value. This means that 4.99 regular acts given in the XML would
in fact be rounded to 4 acts. This affects all calibration runs. Graphs in the
calibration paper have been changed to reflect this bug. Moving forward we can
fix this bug and simply modify the calibration runs to have this same
truncation. This bug is also not yet fixed to ensure reproducibility.


Dynamic rollout proportion:
---------------------------
As a result of the multi-month testing and treatment cascade required for
someone to move from being untreated to treated, it is difficult to predict
exactly how many people will be treated. What we call targetRolloutProportion
is effectively the number of people who have “access to treatment” (i.e. have
started the testing and treatment cascade). Some people in this cascade may
have access but may not be tested immediately. They may also be tested but
receive a false negative result. The issue arises when these people take up
treatment slots but never actually start treatment. 

To remedy this, we have introduced a new concept called dynamic rollout
proportion. When this feature is enabled, a correction factor is calculated as
the number of people accessing treatment divided by the number of people on
treatment. The number of people accessing treatment is then increased by the
same factor. Assuming that this factor remains relatively constant, this
ensures that the correct number of people actually receives treatment.

Because the correct correction factor might change over time, there is a
variable feedback period defined as an input parameter given in months. At the
end of the period, the factor is adjusted up or down as needed. An appropriate
value for this parameter depends on the duration of the testing and treatment
cascade. If people are tested often, it might be a short period. It should
depend on the average amount of time between someone receiving access to
treatment and actually starting treatment (or being lost in the interim).


Template Parameters:
--------------------
Glossed over in the previous section was the fact that interventions need to be
linked with the parameter it is to change. We considered several methods for
making this link before deciding on “template parameters”. This method seemed
preferable to keeping a mapping of arbitrary keys and parameters and is less
unwieldy in the Excel input sheet interface.

Template parameters can be added anywhere in the XML between an opening and
closing tag. As an example:

<proportionHighRiskNonCsw>0.1</proportionHighRiskNonCsw> 

becomes

<proportionHighRiskNonCsw>
  <parameter key=”$proportion_high_risk”>0.1</parameter>
</proportionHighRiskNonCsw> 

In this case 0.1 remains the default value. “$proportion_high_risk” becomes the
key which can be used to refer to this parameter in the interventions section.
It is an arbitrary value (i.e. we could have called it “$1”). If we wanted to
change this value during the model execution, we could create a population
intervention like this:

<generalInterventions>
  <intervention key=”$proportion_high_risk” time=”700” value=”0.2” />
</generalInterventions>

This would have the effect of changing proportion high risk of non-CSWs to 20%
at month 700.


Technical Changes:
------------------

- All relases now follow semantic versioning. This release is version 3.4.0
while the previous was version 3.34c (i.e. 3.34.2).

- Separated HighResolutionTimer class into a POSIX version and a Windows
version with the same interface.

- All instances of NULL are now nullptr.

- The first step was being skipped leading to missing headers in trace files.
This has been fixed.

- Prevent the initiator of a partnership from choosing himself. This wasn't an
issue in the past as men could only choose women. This is in preparation for
the MSM model.

- All arrays of string constants have been changed to vectors.

- A selector in population initialization had been accidentally changed to
selecting homosexual men leading to an erroneous population state. This has
been corrected.

- Ticpp has been replaced with pugixml. Ticpp is not currently being
maintained, is somewhat verbose, and is more prone to errors. Additionally,
pugixml allows for the use of XPath queries. It is generally more pleasant to
use.

- All existing documentation comments have been changed to XML documentation
style. As most development is done in Visual Studio, this allows documentation
to be displayed in the IDE. The consistent style can be parsed and translated
to another style if need be.

- Several unused functions/methods have been removed.

- Some Person methods have been made pure virtual to ensure that the must be
implemented by all child classes.

- Classes have been renamed to reduce the use of abbreviations. Code is read
more than it is written and so this should have little effect on development
while making the code more readable.

- A previous programmer preferred to use this-> unconditionally when referring
to instance variables. This is generally regarded as bad programming style and
has been reverted except when parameter names clash with instance variable
names. Eventually these clashes should be resolved by renaming parameters and
instance variable (i.e. instance variables should have underscore
suffix).

- Some c-style arrays have been changed to STL arrays. These containers are
preferable for many reasons which will not be described here. This transition
is not yet completed.

- All enumerations are in the process of being converted to strongly-typed
enumerations (a new feature in c++11). This transition is not yet
completed.

- Heap allocations are in the process of being converted to RAII-style stack
allocations. This process is ongoing. Those that cannot be easily changed are
being converted to smart pointers.

- "using namespace std" has been removed from the entire codebase. All
references to STL containers and functions have been given a std::
prefix.

- Methods that don't change the enclosing class have been marked const. There
may remain more that can be changed.

- Methods taking no arguments should not take have a void in their parameter
list. This is a C artifact. This has been corrected.

- Classes are in the process of being made more testable. This change includes
reducing the use of friend classes, using dependency injection, and generally
reducing coupling.

- As they are discovered, for loops that iterate over a container using an
incrementing index are being switched to range-based fors or std::algorithms
where possible.

- All include guards are now #pragma once as modern compilers now support this
universally.

- Boost libraries have been replaced by std:: equivalents whenever possible.
Homogenous tuples have been replaced by std::arrays and lexical_cast has been
replaced by std::stoi and related functions.

- All Graphviz functionality has been removed. Eventually every person will
keep track of any important life events (time of birth, time of death, time of
infection, etc.). This data can be transformed into JSON to be used by any
library for visualization.

- XMLUtil was no longer being used so it has been removed.

- A bug was discovered in ART rollout that made people moving from
Population::rankedForTreatment to Population::rolloutTreatedContext to be
double counted. This only affects trace files (not the simulation itself). This
was caused by iterating over a copy rather than a reference to the actual
ranking bucket.

- WxWidgets is now linked statically so that DLLs do not have to be distributed
with the executable.

- The project build system is now created programatically using premake version
5. This helps keep build environments in sync thereby ensuring that the model
can be built on any combination of platform and compiler as needed.

- All possible warnings have been enabled along with fatal warnings. The
project will no longer build with any errors present. This required fixing a
large number of minor problems (mostly signed/unsigned problems). The code is
now being checked in gcc 4.8, clang5, and VS2013 compilers for
warnings.

- The project is now built with both cepac4.4a and cepac4.5b which can be
switched as desired.

Targeted Interventions:
-----------------------
Future investigations require changing a parameter on a particular group of
people rather than the entire population. This can be thought of as a general
“behavioral intervention”. An example of such an intervention would be
increasing condom usage among males of a certain age or reducing the chance of
becoming a sex worker for young women (and only young women).

This feature was implemented as part of the larger feature to replace the old
sequencing method (creating an XML for various time points and reloading the
XML at those times during the execution of the simulation). This feature
enabled a parameter to be changed at a certain time. By coopting these
parameters and adding an optional target to them, we now have the ability to
target not just treatment intervention but also behavioral interventions. 

Here is a list of all possible interventions (the names aren’t significant
except inasmuch as they identify the parameter in the XML file):

| parameters                                  | Can affect
+---------------------------------------------+---------------------
| BirthRate                                   | newborns
| ProportionMale                              | newborns
| ProportionCircumcised                       | targeted
| AgeSexualDebutYears                         | targeted
| AssortativenessSteady                       | population
| AssortativenessRegular                      | population
| AssortativenessCasual                       | population
| AssortativenessCsw                          | population
| MaleChanceBecomeSexWorker                   | population
| MalePartnerAcqMultWithSteadyHigh            | targeted
| MalePartnerAcqMultWithSteadyLow             | targeted
| MaleSteadyAcquisitionRateHighMean           | targeted
| MaleSteadyAcquisitionRateHighStdDev         | targeted
| MaleSteadyAcquisitionRateLowMean            | targeted
| MaleSteadyAcquisitionRateLowStdDev          | targeted
