If a parameter can affect “population” then it cannot have a target. It will
change that parameter for everyone in the population at the time it is changed.
If a parameter can affect “newborns” then it will have no effect on existing
people, but people born into the population will have be governed by this
parameter. Finally, if a parameter can affect “targeted” then it may have a
target specified in addition to its value.

In the XML, a general intervention looks like:

<chanceCondomUsePerEventHighRisk>
  <distribution type="normal">
    <mean><parameter key="$0">0.5</parameter></mean>
    <stdDev><parameter key="$1">0.25<parameter></stdDev>
  </distribution>
</chanceCondomUsePerEventHighRisk>
…
<generalInterventions>
  <intervention time="834" key="$0" value="0.75" />
  <intervention time="834" key="$1" value="0.25" />
</generalInterventions>

This has the effect of increasing the chance of any male in the population in
the given partnership type using a condom on or after month 834.

<generalInterventions>
  <intervention time="834" key="$0" value="0.75" target-age="17-19" />
  <intervention time="834" key="$1" value="0.25" target-age="17-19" />
</generalInterventions>

This has the same effect as above with the added restriction only males that
are between the ages of 17 and 19 inclusive at month 834 will be affected. Note
that they will maintain this increased condom usage throughout their lives
unless it is changed by another targeted intervention. Also, note that both
values must be changed at the same time. The simulation will stop if one is set
but not the other. This restriction may be lifted in the future but is
currently required because the normal distribution is converted to a beta
distribution and both parameters must be known for this calculation. This
applies to all of the distribution-based parameters.

Here is a table of the possible targets:

| Attribute                      | Value                                         |
+--------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+
| target-age                     | age range in years in the form                |
|                                | “lower-upper”(inclusive)                      |
| target-gender                  | male or female                                |
| targer-sexual-orientation      | hetero or homo                                |
| target-sexual-activity-status  | NA (non-sexually-active) or (sexual-acrive)   |
| target-relationship-status     | single or non-single                          |
| target-employment              | csw or non-csw                                |
| target-hiv-status              | uninfected, unobserved-chronic,               |
|                                | unobserved-primary, unobserved-late-stage,    |
|                                | observed-chronic, observed-primary,           |
|                                | observed-late-stage                           |
| target-treatmenr-status        | treated or unreated                           |

One or more of these can be specified for any general intervention.

Version 3.33b
=============

Sequence ART Rollout Eligibility
--------------------------------

Problem:

During the course of several simulations, it became necessary to change ART
rollout eligibility at a certain time point. It was determined that, although
these criteria were changed in sequenced XML files, the eligibility was not
changing as no significant change in the “number eligible for access to
treatment” was visible in the ART rollout outcome file.

Reviewing the code for loading sequenced XML files indicated that the XML
parameters outside of simulation/population were simply not being read and
couldn’t therefore be sequenced in the model at that time.

Solution:

The functionality already existed for loading ART rollout eligibility from the
XML. This code was modularized into a new function in the Sim class,
updateEligibility, which is called when the sequenced XML changes. This
function loads the new eligibility allowing eligibility for access to treatment
to change as desired at the correct time.


Version 3.33a
=============

Singleperson Prevalent Trace
----------------------------

Problem:

Functionality already existed to trace a number of newborns at a certain time
and a number of people in each age group from simulation start. It would be
interesting to see the early dynamics of the epidemic by viewing the
singleperson trace of the initial prevalent population. This was previously
only possible by running several simulations and being lucky.

Solution:

A new input was added to the XML, “tracePrevalentCases” as a child element of
“simulation”. When this value is 0, prevalent cases will not be traced in the
singleperson trace file. Conversely, when this is set to 1, they will be
traced. The value of of simulation/writeTrace/singleperson supersedes this
value. Thus, if the entire singleperson trace file is disabled, the prevalent
cases will not be traced regardless of the value of “tracePrevalentCases”.


Exposure Counting
----------------- 

Bug:

During the simulation of a person’s sexual activity, the infection tracker
records any exposure that occurs between serodiscordant partners. This
recording was being done outside the per act loop and thus only counted one
exposure for a given month even if many exposures occurred.

Bug fixed:

Move 
  infTrack->recordExposure(_eventParams.currTime, infected); 
inside 
  for(int i = 0; i < _numActs; i++) 
in 
  "Person::sexualActivity" function.


RNG Seed Parameter
------------------

Problem:

On GUI simulation runs, fixed seed could be enabled or disabled using the
“fixed seed” checkbox. On console runs (e.g. on the cluster), this could only
be done through recompiling the entire executable with the parameter passed to
Sim changed. It would be more convenient to be able to specify a specific fixed
seed or disable fixed seed through the XML.

Solution:

A new element was added to the XML as a child of the “simulation” element
called “fixedSeed”. It can be one of three given values:

  -1 – Disable fixed seed and seed random number generator from current system clock
   0 – Use default fixed seed (currently 36291434)
  >0 – Use the given positive number less than 2^31-1


Shifted Outcome Sheet
---------------------

Description:

This is a new trace file which summarizes a number of important statistics
about the population on a yearly basis shifted by the given parameter
“monthOf1990” as calculated during phase 3 calibration. These outcomes are
recorded:

- Year relative to month of 1990
- Sexually active population size
- Number of incident cases
- Number of prevalent cases at year start
- Prevalence among sexually active population
- Incidence
- Number of HIV tests
- Number of tests stratified by result (true positive, false positive, true
  negative, false negative)
- Number eligible for access to treatment
- Number accessing treatment
- Number eligible for treatment
- Number receiving treatment


ART Rollout Outcome Sheet
-------------------------

Definitions:

- CEPAC simulation context – the CEPAC IN file which is currently applied to a
  CDM person’s internal “CEPAC patient”

- CEPAC untreated context – the IN file which is initially applied to the
  general population and may include testing but should not generally include ARV
  treatment

- CEPAC treated context – the IN file which a person switches to when they meet
  certain criteria and there are available treatment slots

- Treatment slot – a space for a single person in CDM to move to a CEPAC
  treated context. This is necessary but not sufficient for actually receiving
  treatment (e.g. if the person does not also meet CEPAC ART start criteria)

- Eligible for access to treatment – the person in CDM meets any of the five
  ranked criteria specified in the CDM XML

Description:

Statistics about ART rollout were not being tracked in the model previously. It
was decided that the following outcomes should be recorded at each month:

- Number of HIV tests offered, accepted, returned for result
- Number of HIV tests
- Number of people eligible for access to treatment (in CDM)
- Number of people accessing treatment
- Number of people eligible for treatment (in CEPAC)
- Number of people being treated

Additionally, these tallies should be stratified by the following demographic
profiles (except for tests offered, accepted, returned for result which are for
the entire population):

- Gender (male, female)

- Gender and sexual activity and age group (male/NA/all, male/SA/0-16,
  male/SA/17-19, male/SA/20-24, male/SA/30-34, male/SA/35-39, male/SA/40-44,
  male/SA/45-49, male/SA/50-100, female/NA/all, female/SA/0-16, female/SA/17-19,
  female/SA/20-24, female/SA/30-34, female/SA/35-39, female/SA/40-44,
  female/SA/45-49, female/SA/50-100)

- CD4 Stratum (very low, low, medium low, medium high, high, very high)

- Risk group (Non-CSW Low-Risk Male, Non-CSW Low-Risk Female, Non-CSW High-Risk
  Male, Non-CSW High-Risk Female, CSW Low-Risk Female, CSW High-Risk
  Female)

- (HIV tests only) Test result (true positive, false positive, true negative,
  false negative)


Version 3.32k
=============

ART Rollout Bug
---------------

Problem:

With a fixed seed defined, the random number generator will always produce the
same sequence of random numbers. This allows us to run multiple simulations
with the same parameters and get the same results. Unfortunately, it became
apparent that simulation runs with ART rollout intervention enabled differed
(in prevalence and other important statistics) from runs without well before
the intervention actually started. Comparing the number of random numbers
generated every month indicated that extra random numbers were generated for
each incident case with ART rollout enabled. As an example:

| Time       | Incident | Without ART Rollout | With ART Rollout               |
+------------+----------+---------------------+--------------------------------+
| 599        | 0        | 85, 11, 83, 74, 89  | 85, 11, 83, 74, 89             |
| 600 (prev. | 6        | 89, 86, 19, 76, 61  | 89, 86, 19, 76, 61, 56, 91, 73 |
| delay)     |          |                     | 69, 57, 96                     |
| 601        | 1        | 56, 91, 73, 69, 57  | 5, 89, 42, 15, 63              |
| 602        | 1        | 96,  5, 89, 42, 15  | 52, 75, 58, 13, 8, 13          |
| ...        |          |                     |                                |

It can be seen that the same sequence of random numbers are being generated,
but because they are shifted and used for different calculations, different
results must be produced.
Tracing program execution indicated that these extra random numbers were used
as random indices for “rolloutUntreatedPool” in Population.cpp, a list which
tracks all of those people who have been infected but aren’t yet accessing
treatment. The shuffling was necessary to ensure that order of insertion into
the list didn’t bias those that were infected earlier or later.

Bug Fix:

Delaying the shuffling of the "rolloutUntreatedPool" until the time that people
can actually begin receiving ART was sufficient for solving this issue. The
original way might be compared with shuffling a deck of cards and then taking
the top card whenever a random card is needed. The new way is more like leaving
the cards in a possibly biased order and selecting a random card from any place
in the deck when a random card is needed.
